de)", "ECG abnormality (Tier-1 unresolved image reference)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Acute Coronary Syndrome / Myocardial Infarction", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.015838573261320623, "danger": 1.0, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Positional chest pain more commonly seen in pericardial disease than MI, but absence of ECG changes makes MI less likely. This strengthens pericardial pathology while excluding MI."]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Pericardial Pathology / Cardiac Tamponade", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.5116954072050538, "posterior": 0.5766745331580532, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Positional changes in chest pain (E4) and syncope (E5) provide moderate support for pericardial pathology (B2). Normal heart sounds (E13) slightly limit this association.", "Classic positional relief of chest pain (E4) provides strong support for pericardial pathology (B2). This contrasts with MI (B1), which lacks positional pain features (E2)."], "evidence_against": ["Normal heart sounds (E13) provide strong evidence against cardiac tamponade (B2). Early-stage tamponade cannot fully explain positional symptoms without muffled S1/S2 (E13)."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Does the patient have pulsus paradoxus or neck vein distension?", "Is the pain relieved by sitting up/forward?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Echocardiogram for effusion", "ECG analysis for diffuse ST elevation"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Cardiac Arrhythmia / Myopathy", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.019798216576650776, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of asymmetric septal hypertrophy on exam weakens HOCM’s role in explaining syncope, but exertional nature of syncope still allows alternative arrhythmias. HOCM remains less likely."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Metabolic / Channelopathy-Related Cardiac Arrest", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.031677146522641246, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Acute Pericarditis with Effusion", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.519584395837623, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Positional chest pain more commonly seen in pericardial disease than MI, but absence of ECG changes makes MI less likely. This strengthens pericardial pathology while excluding MI.", "Absence of asymmetric septal hypertrophy on exam weakens HOCM’s role in explaining syncope, but exertional nature of syncope still allows alternative arrhythmias. HOCM remains less likely."], "evidence_against": ["Lack of fever/viral illness doesn't strongly confirm pericarditis, but positional pain remains more supportive of pericardial pathology than tamponade. ECG resolution remains critical."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1.1", "B2.1.2", "B2.1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there fever or recent viral illness?", "Does the patient have Kussmaul's sign on jugular venous pulse assessment?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["ECG for diffuse ST elevation (pericarditis pattern)", "Echocardiogram for pericardial effusion and thickening"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Cardiac Tamponade (Early Stage)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.04105358189334305, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Cardiac tamponade requires time for effusion to develop to critical levels; acute 30-minute presentation contradicts this mechanism, moderately weakening the case."]}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Constrictive Pericarditis", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.016036555427087127, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The absence of chronic symptoms (E2) significantly contradicts constrictive pericarditis, providing strong evidence against this subacute/chronic entity."]}, "B2.1.1": {"id": "B2.1.1", "label": "Viral Acute Pericarditis with Effusion", "parent": "B2.1", "level": 3, "status": "live", "prior": 0.31175063750257376, "posterior": 0.31175063750257376, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Does the patient have a history of recent viral illness or upper respiratory infection?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["CBC with CRP/ESR (viral pattern vs bacterial neutrophilia)", "ECG with attention to diffuse PR depression and ST elevation"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.6, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.0, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B2.1.2": {"id": "B2.1.2", "label": "Acute Suppurative (Bacterial) Pericarditis", "parent": "B2.1", "level": 3, "status": "live", "prior": 0.12989609895940574, "posterior": 0.12989609895940574, "danger": 1.0, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Does the patient have systemic signs of infection (fever, leukocytosis)?"]