": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 5, "bundle_size": 9, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Would presence of fever or recent viral illness (not mentioned in E1-E15) provide stronger confirmation for pericarditis over tamponade (B2.2)?", "result_summary": "Lack of fever/viral illness doesn't strongly confirm pericarditis, but positional pain remains more supportive of pericardial pathology than tamponade. ECG resolution remains critical.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2.1": "weak_against", "B2.2": "moderate_for", "B2.3": "strong_against", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 6, "bundle_size": 9, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the 30-minute time course (E2) conflict with cardiac tamponade (B2.2) given its requirement for subacute/pericardial effusion development?", "result_summary": "Cardiac tamponade requires time for effusion to develop to critical levels; acute 30-minute presentation contradicts this mechanism, moderately weakening the case.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2.1": "neutral", "B2.2": "moderate_against", "B2.3": "strong_against", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 7, "bundle_size": 9, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Would chronic/progressive symptoms (not described in vignette) provide stronger diagnostic alignment for constrictive pericarditis (B2.3) against acute presentation (E2)?", "result_summary": "The absence of chronic symptoms (E2) significantly contradicts constrictive pericarditis, providing strong evidence against this subacute/chronic entity.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2.1": "neutral", "B2.2": "neutral", "B2.3": "strong_against", "B3": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 3, "bundle_id": 3, "bundle_position": 8, "bundle_size": 9, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does absence of asymmetric septal hypertrophy from initial auscultation (E13) disfavor arrhythmic causes like HOCM (B3) despite exertional syncope (E5)?", "result_summary": "Absence of asymmetric septal hypertrophy on exam weakens HOCM’s role in explaining syncope, but exertional nature of syncope still allows alternative arrhythmias. HOCM remains less likely.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_for", "B2.1": "moderate_for", "B2.2": "neutral", "B2.3": "neutral", "B3": "weak_against"}}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 1, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of ST-segment changes or other acute infarct patterns in the referenced ECG (E14) rule out myocardial infarction (B1) as a life-threatening differentiator from pericardial causes?", "result_summary": "The absence of ST-segment elevation or other acute infarct patterns in the ECG moderately weakens the likelihood of myocardial infarction (B1) as the cause of syncope. This supports pericardial pathology as the competing differential but does not confirm a specific subtype (B2.1 or B2.2) due to unresolved ECG findings."}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.16194331983805668, "B2": 0.6558704453441295, "B3": 0.10121457489878542, "B4": 0.08097165991902834, "B2.1": 0.409919028340081, "B2.2": 0.16396761133603238, "B2.3": 0.08198380566801619}, {"B1": 0.045529565761766554, "B2": 0.5116954072050538, "B3": 0.028455978601104093, "B4": 0.045529565761766554, "B2.1": 0.4148881680040977, "B2.2": 0.07375789653406181, "B2.3": 0.023049342666894315}, {"B1": 0.015838573261320623, "B2": 0.05709013732043018, "B3": 0.019798216576650776, "B4": 0.031677146522641246, "B2.1": 0.519584395837623, "B2.2": 0.04105358189334305, "B2.3": 0.016036555427087127, "B2.1.1": 0.31175063750257376, "B2.1.2": 0.12989609895940574, "B2.1.3": 0.07793765937564344}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Syncope following acute chest pain and positional relief pattern suggest pericardial tamponade and myocardial infarction as time-critical differentials requiring immediate ECG confirmation.", "required_actions": ["Perform rapid 12-lead ECG analysis to identify pericardial signs (diffuse PR depression, ST elevation) or STEMI (locally concave ST elevation, reciprocal changes)", "Assess for pulsus paradoxus and jugular venous distension (JVP) to evaluate tamponade risk", "Initiate cardiac monitoring due to syncope and tachycardia; consider arrhythmia monitoring"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Interrupt is active (emergency override potential) but pending ECG analysis is critical for high-risk cardiac differentials (STEMI vs pericardial disease). B2.1 remains top terminal branch at posterior 0.41 < 0.75. Dangerous branches B1 (MI) and B2.1 (pericarditis) require further investigation per actionable parent rule violation."}, "turn_budget_used": 4, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.92, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.31175063750257376, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_vignette": "A 50-year-old man presents to the emergency department. He states that the pain is dull in quality, started 30 minutes ago, has been gradually worsening, and is worse with exertion. He also endorses some shortness of breath. He also believes the pain is worse when leaning back and improved when leaning forward. His wife noticed he fainted after the pain started but regained consciousness shortly after. He has a medical history of diabetes and hypertension for which he is on metformin and losartan. His temperature is 99.2°F (37.3°C), blood pressure is 130/87 mmHg, pulse is 99/min, respirations are 22/min, and oxygen saturation is 100% on room air. Physical exam reveals an overweight man. A normal S1 and S2 are auscultated with clear breath sounds. An ECG is performed as seen in Figure A.", "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Hyperkalemia"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Hypokalemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Myocardial infarction"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Pericarditis"}], "static_evidence_items": [{"id": "E1", "kind": "direct", "content": "Age: 50-year-old man", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E2", "kind": "direct", "content": "Chief complaint: chest pain (dull, began 30 minutes ago, gradually worsening, worse with exertion)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E3", "kind": "direct", "content": "Symptoms: shortness of breath", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E4", "kind": "direct", "content": "Chest pain positional features: worse when leaning back, improves when leaning forward", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E5", "kind": "direct", "content": "History of syncope: witnessed fainting episode after chest pain onset, regained consciousness shortly after", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E6", "kind": "direct", "content": "Past medical history: diabetes and hypertension", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E7", "kind": "direct", "content": "Current medications: metformin and losartan", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E8", "kind": "direct", "content": "Temperature: 99.2°F (37.3°C)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E9", "kind": "direct", "content": "Blood pressure: 130/87 mmHg", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E10", "kind": "direct", "content": "Pulse: 99/min (tachycardia)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E11", "kind": "direct", "content": "Respirations: 22/min (tachypnea)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E12", "kind": "direct", "content": "Oxygen saturation: 100% on room air", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E13", "kind": "direct", "content": "Physical examination findings: overweight male with normal heart sounds (S1/S2) and normal lung sounds (clear breath sounds)", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E14", "kind": "image_reference", "content": "ECG shown in Figure A — image not available in text form", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E15", "kind": "derived", "content": "Positional chest pain (worse reclining, better upright) and history of syncope suggest pericardial pathology", "source_ids": ["E2", "E4", "E5"], "independent": false, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Ventricular septal hypertrophy', 'Acute coronary syndrome', 'Myocardial infarction', 'Asymmetric septal hypertrophy'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=83%, source=both]\n\nAcute Coronary Syndrome / Myocardial Infarction vs Cardiac Arrhythmia / Myopathy:\n  Favours first:  abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebellum morphology, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal facial shape, abnormal foot morphology\n  Favours second: blurry vision, fainting, palpitations, rapid heartbeat, slow heartbeat\n\nAcute Coronary Syndrome / Myocardial Infarction vs Cardiac Tamponade (Early Stage):\n  Favours first:  abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebellum morphology, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal facial shape, abnormal foot morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal pericardium morphology, chylopericardium, clammy and cold extremities, confusion, decreased urine output\n\nAcute Coronary Syndrome / Myocardial Infarction vs Viral Acute Pericarditis with Effusion:\n  Favours first:  abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebellum morphology, abnormal facial shape, abnormal foot morphology, abnormal globus pallidus morphology\n  Favours second: abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level, abnormal prolactin level\n\nAcute Coronary Syndrome / Myocardial Infarction vs Acute Suppurative (Bacterial) Pericarditis:\n  Favours first:  abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebellum morphology, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal facial shape, abnormal foot morphology\n  Favours second: cough, fever, hypotension, pain improved by sitting up, pain worsened by lying down\n\nCardiac Arrhythmia / Myopathy vs Cardiac Tamponade (Early Stage):\n  Favours first:  blurry vision, dizziness, fainting, lightheadedness, palpitations\n  Favours second: abnormal pericardium morphology, chylopericardium, clammy and cold extremities, confusion, decreased urine output\n\nCardiac Arrhythmia / Myopathy vs Viral Acute Pericarditis with Effusion:\n  Favours first:  blurry vision, dizziness, fainting, lightheadedness, palpitations\n  Favours second: abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level\n\nCardiac Arrhythmia / Myopathy vs Acute Suppurative (Bacterial) Pericarditis:\n  Favours first:  anxiety, blurry vision, chest pain, dizziness, fainting\n  Favours second: cough, fatigue, fever, hypotension, pain improved by sitting up\n\nCardiac Tamponade (Early Stage) vs Viral Acute Pericarditis with Effusion:\n  Favours first:  abnormal pericardium morphology, chylopericardium, clammy and cold extremities, confusion, decreased urine output\n  Favours second: abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level\n\nCardiac Tamponade (Early Stage) vs Acute Suppurative (Bacterial) Pericarditis:\n  Favours first:  abnormal pericardium morphology, anxiety, chest pain, chylopericardium, clammy and cold extremities\n  Favours second: cough, fatigue, fever, pain improved by sitting up, pain worsened by lying down\n\nViral Acute Pericarditis with Effusion vs Acute Suppurative (Bacterial) Pericarditis:\n  Favours first:  abnormal basal ganglia mri signal intensity, abnormal central motor function, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal ekg, abnormal growth hormone level\n  Favours second: cough, hypotension, pain improved by sitting up, pain worsened by lying down, palpitations\n\nRelated to Acute Coronary Syndrome / Myocardial Infarction: myocardial ischemia, syndromic disease\n\nRelated to Cardiac Tamponade (Early Stage): pericardial effusion (disease)\n\nRelated to Viral Acute Pericarditis with Effusion: infectious disease, pericarditis (disease)\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  He states that the pain is dull in quality → abnormality of pain sensation → Acute Coronary Syndrome / Myocardial Infarction"}
